    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've been looking at the cross-sections as well. When I look at the fracture points of these broken pieces, the breakage patterns are really erratic. Some clusters of shards seem almost purposefully scattered or disrupted in a way that doesn't quite align with the typical heavy fragmentation we see in this stratum. Are there specific microscopic indicators in the clay matrix or the slip interface that could tell me if a shard was broken by sudden thermal shock during the original firing versus post-depositional damage?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The microscopic examination of the clay matrix and slip interface can provide clues. Sudden thermal shock-induced breakage often leaves behind fine, interconnected cracks or small cavities at or just below the surface, especially if the breakage occurred before the glaze or slip fully set. In contrast, post-depositional damage may result in a more uniform or discontinuous breakage pattern, often with a greater proportion of primarily thermal stress-induced cleavage planes running perpendicular to the original surface. Careful observation of these microscopic details can help you distinguish between original firing damage and post-depositional breakage.",
      "source": "target",
